<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oder–Neisse line</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Oder–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nysa">Neisse</a> line is the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, following the Oder and Lusatian Neisse rivers. Agreed provisionally at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potsdam">Potsdam</a> Conference in 1945, it placed the former German lands to the east under Polish and Soviet administration, and millions of Germans were expelled from them. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">East Germany</a> recognised it in 1950 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a> in 1970, and reunited Germany confirmed it by treaty in 1990.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oder-neisse-line.jpg" alt="Oder–Neisse line" width="320" height="207" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oder-neisse.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Oder–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nysa">Neisse</a> line is an unofficial term for the modern border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. The line generally follows the Oder and Lusatian Neisse rivers and meets the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a> in the north. A small portion of Polish territory lies west of it, including the cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szczecin">Szczecin</a> and Świnoujście, known in German as Stettin and Swinemünde. The border is 472 km long, the shortest possible between the two countries, and it divides several cities in two, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorlitz">Görlitz</a> and Zgorzelec and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> and Słubice.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The lower Oder in Silesia was the western border of Piast Poland from the 10th until the 13th century, and from about the time of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> some proposed restoring that line. At the Tehran Conference in late 1943 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a> raised the extension of Poland's western frontier to the Oder, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franklin-d-roosevelt">Franklin D. Roosevelt</a> agreed in general that it should reach that river. At Yalta in February 1945 the Soviets for the first time openly supported a frontier on the Western rather than the Eastern Neisse. Winston Churchill objected that it would be a pity to stuff the Polish goose so full of German food that it got indigestion.</p><p>The question was left to the Potsdam Conference. On 2 August 1945 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potsdam-agreement">Potsdam Agreement</a> placed the German territories east of the line under Polish administrative control, in anticipation of a final peace treaty, and decided that Germans remaining there should be expelled. Silesia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a> and the southern part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-prussia">East Prussia</a> went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>; northern East Prussia, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Königsberg</a> renamed Kaliningrad, went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>. Much of the German population, estimated at 12 million in autumn 1944, had already fled the advancing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a>. In Poland the territories were called the Recovered Territories, a term widely used in the propaganda of the People's Republic of Poland.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">East Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>'s Communist government signed the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorlitz">Zgorzelec</a> in 1950, recognising the line as the Border of Peace and Friendship. West Germany under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konrad-adenauer">Konrad Adenauer</a> refused to accept it as permanent, and later accepted it with reservations in a treaty with Poland. After the revolutions of 1989 Poland and the reunified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> confirmed the line in the border treaty of 1990, ratified in 1992.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Oder–Neisse line” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1945 the lands east of the <b>Oder–Neisse line</b> went to Poland and the Soviet Union and an estimated twelve million Germans fled or were expelled; Control Council Law No.</blockquote><a href="/empire/free-state-of-prussia">Free State of Prussia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Germany was divided into four occupation zones, Berlin into four sectors, and the lands east of the <b>Oder-Neisse line</b> passed to Polish and Soviet administration.</blockquote><a href="/state/allied-occupied-germany">Allied-occupied Germany</a></li><li><blockquote>Industry was nationalised, and German territory east of the <b>Oder–Neisse line</b> was added to Poland.</blockquote><a href="/state/polish-peoples-republic">Polish People's Republic</a></li></ul></section>
